---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { findInsight } from '../../insights';

const insight = findInsight('first-corporate-tax-return-what-breaks')!;
---

<Article
  insight={insight}
  standfirst="Registration is the easy part. The failures we see are upstream — a chart of accounts that cannot support a tax computation, and adjustments nobody can evidence twelve months later."
>
  <p>
    Every first Corporate Tax cycle we have been near has followed the same
    shape. The registration goes fine. The deadline is known. Somebody has read
    the guidance. And then the actual computation takes four times longer than
    anyone budgeted, for reasons that have nothing to do with tax technical
    knowledge.
  </p>

  <p>
    The problems are structural, and they were already there before the regime
    arrived. Corporate Tax simply made them visible.
  </p>

  <h2>The chart of accounts was built for management reporting</h2>

  <p>
    Most owner-managed businesses have a chart of accounts that grew
    organically. Accounts were added when somebody needed to see a number
    separately, and never rationalised. There is a general expense account
    holding several unrelated categories, three accounts that mean roughly the
    same thing, and a suspense account with a balance nobody will claim.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is survivable for management reporting, where you mostly care about
    trends. It is not survivable for a tax computation, where you need to say
    with confidence which balances contain items that are not deductible. If
    entertainment sits inside a general overhead account alongside genuinely
    deductible costs, the honest answer to "how much of this is disallowed" is a
    guess — and a guess is exactly what you cannot defend.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What to do about it:</strong> rationalise before you compute, not
    after. Split the accounts that will need splitting, and do it at a level that
    survives a question. This is unglamorous work and it is almost always the
    critical path.
  </p>

  <h2>The adjustments exist, but the reasoning does not</h2>

  <p>
    The second failure is subtler. A competent person works through the trial
    balance, decides what needs adding back, and produces a computation that is
    very likely correct. Twelve months later, nobody can reconstruct why a
    particular figure was chosen.
  </p>

  <p>
    We have seen a computation with a single line reading "adjustment — per
    review" against a material amount. It was almost certainly right. It was also
    completely undefendable, because the reasoning lived in a conversation.
  </p>

  <p>
    An adjustment needs three things recorded alongside it: what it relates to,
    why it was made, and what evidence supports the amount. If any of the three
    only exists in someone's head, you have a liability that matures the moment
    that person leaves or the question gets asked.
  </p>

  <h2>The zero band creates a false sense of comfort</h2>

  <p>
    Taxable income up to AED 375,000 is taxed at 0%, and Small Business Relief
    is available where revenue does not exceed AED 3,000,000 for the period.
    Both are genuine reliefs and plenty of businesses fall within them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The trap is treating "we will not owe anything" as equivalent to "we do not
    need to do the work". The computation still has to be produced. The position
    still has to be supportable. And a business sitting just under a threshold
    this year is a business that may cross it next year, at which point the
    absence of a working method is discovered under deadline rather than at
    leisure.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cheapest year to build the process is the year the answer is zero.
  </p>

  <h2>Nobody owns the timetable</h2>

  <p>
    Filing dates derive from the financial year end, which means they are
    knowable years in advance. In practice they are held in one person's calendar
    or in a shared spreadsheet that is accurate for the entity somebody last
    looked at.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a single company this is merely risky. For a firm with a portfolio it is
    the dominant operational problem: a hundred entities with different year
    ends, each generating its own obligations, tracked by hand. Deadlines should
    be generated from the year end, not maintained.
  </p>

  <h2>What good looks like</h2>

  <p>
    A first cycle that goes well tends to have four things in place:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A chart of accounts mapped once to both statutory presentation and tax
      classification, so the statements and the computation come from the same
      source and cannot disagree.
    </li>
    <li>
      An adjustment library — your own list, with the reasoning and evidence
      attached to each item, versioned so a prior year keeps the rules that
      applied when it was filed.
    </li>
    <li>
      A computation that reconciles back to the trial balance it started from,
      and tells you if that trial balance has since moved.
    </li>
    <li>
      A period lock, so once something is filed it is frozen and later
      corrections become explicit prior-period adjustments rather than silent
      edits to history.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of that is exotic. It is the difference between a computation you can
    produce and a computation you can defend, and the gap between the two is
    usually about three weeks of unglamorous preparation done at the right time.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want to see the arithmetic on your own figures first, the
    <a href="/platform/corporate-tax/#estimator">estimator</a> on our Corporate
    Tax page runs in your browser and sends nothing anywhere.
  </p>
</Article>
